          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Cell: The Building Block of Life"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.856,
            0.018,
            0.953,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11 N"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.088,
            0.635,
            0.139
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "parts of an object), and magnification. These improvements have turned microscope into a powerful tool for studying cells."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.142,
            0.635,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You can see a picture of the lower surface of Colocasia leaf observed under a Scanning Electron Microscope (Fig. 2.4)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.19,
            0.393,
            0.211
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2.2 Structure of a Cell"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.647,
            0.097,
            0.958,
            0.22
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.647,
                0.097,
                0.958,
                0.22
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_3_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.214,
            0.635,
            0.266
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You have learnt that cells are organised into specialised tissues and organs, and collectively perform specific function. For these cells to function as units, they must"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.645,
            0.226,
            0.96,
            0.256
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 2.4: Electron micrograph of lower surface of a Colocasia leaf showing stomata"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.265,
            0.775,
            0.35
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "be able to interact with one another and with their surroundings. These interactions occur at the cell boundary, where substances move between the cells and their external environment. Even single-celled organisms exchange materials and respond to their environment through cell membrane."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.358,
            0.704,
            0.378
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2.2.1 Cell membrane — The universal feature of a cell"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.381,
            0.775,
            0.501
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The cell membrane is a thin boundary that surrounds a cell and protects its contents. It defines the individuality of a cell and is also called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "plasma membrane",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". The cell membrane is selectively permeable, which means it allows some substances to pass through it while blocking others. You have learnt how oxygen and carbon dioxide move across the membranes of alveoli in the lungs. How does the structure of the cell membrane in the cells of alveoli control the movement of substances across it?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.781,
            0.436,
            0.852,
            0.47
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Grade 7",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Curiosity "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 9",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.509,
            0.431,
            0.527
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 2.2: Let us experiment"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.529,
            0.479,
            0.582
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. With the help of a kitchen knife, carefully cut a potato into two pieces of roughly equal size (Fig. 2.5)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.583,
            0.482,
            0.633
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Measure and record the initial weight of both the pieces using a weighing balance."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.636,
            0.479,
            0.671
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Put one piece of the potato in Beaker A with plain water."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.672,
            0.479,
            0.723
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Put the other piece of the potato in Beaker B with 20 per cent salt or sugar solution."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.725,
            0.479,
            0.777
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Leave them undisturbed for about an hour or until you notice a visible change in the size of the pieces."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.778,
            0.479,
            0.813
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. Measure and record the final weight of each piece."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.814,
            0.479,
            0.849
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. Calculate the difference between the initial and their final weights."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.85,
            0.478,
            0.884
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What do you observe? You may observe that —"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.888,
            0.436,
            0.906
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Beaker A —",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The potato piece swells."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.908,
            0.445,
            0.927
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Beaker B —",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The potato piece shrinks."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
